## 4. Library & platform compatibility (this is the real blocker)

### Spring ecosystem

* **Spring Boot officially targets LTS releases**
* Java 25 support will be **experimental at best**
* Expect:

  * Broken bytecode enhancement
  * Reflection edge cases
  * AOT/native regressions

➡️ **Spring Boot 3.x is tuned for Java 17 & 21**

---

### PostgreSQL (Cosmos DB)

* pgjdbc usually lags **1–2 Java releases**
* Java 25 = untested socket + TLS behavior
* Prepared statement caching risk
* Connection pool instability under load

---

### Azure AI SDKs

* Track 2 SDKs lag non-LTS testing
* Netty + Reactor compatibility uncertain
* TLS cipher changes can break inference calls

---

### Observability stack (often forgotten)

These break *first* on non-LTS:

* OpenTelemetry Java agent
* Application Insights
* JFR tooling
* Async profiler
* ByteBuddy / ASM

➡️ A broken agent = blind production system

---

## 5. Security posture: Java 25 vs Java 21

| Aspect            | Java 21 (LTS) | Java 25     |
| ----------------- | ------------- | ----------- |
| CVE patching      | Predictable   | Short-lived |
| Vendor backports  | Yes           | Limited     |
| Library CVE fixes | Fast          | Slow        |
| Compliance audits | Accepted      | Questioned  |
| SOC2 / ISO        | Safe          | Risky       |

**Security teams prefer LTS for a reason.**

---

## 6. Performance & concurrency — the honest truth

Java 25 gives you **nothing material** over Java 21 for your workload:

* Virtual Threads → already stable in **21**
* GC improvements → incremental
* Loom, Panama → already usable
* AI / HTTP / DB workloads → same gains

➡️ **Java 21 = 95% of Java 25 benefits, 10% of the risk**

